Excel Interface

The Excel interface consists of a Ribbon (menu of commands) and a Sheet (grid of rows and columns). 
The Ribbon provides shortcuts to Excel commands (e.g. File, Home, Insert tabs). 
The Sheet is a grid of cells identified by column letters and row numbers – e.g. cell C4 is column C, row 4. 
The Name Box (left of the formula bar) shows the active cell (like C4). Use cell references (A1, B2, etc.) in formulas.

Use Case: Understanding the interface helps navigate data, input formulas, and format sheets.
Interview Q: “How do you refer to cell B3?” – Answer: “By its column-letter and row-number (B3).”
Common Mistakes: Not using $ for absolute references vs. relative. Forgetting the “=” before formulas.
Tip: Double-click the bottom-right corner of a cell (Fill Handle) to auto-fill formulas down.

Basic Formulas: SUM, AVERAGE, etc.
SUM
  The SUM function adds a range of numbers. Syntax: =SUM(number1, [number2], …) or =SUM(A1:A5). 
  For example, =SUM(2,3,5,7,11) returns 28. It ignores text; any non-numeric cell is skipped.
  Example:
  =SUM(A1:A5)
  (If A1–A5 contain 2,3,5,7,11, the result is 28.)

  Use Case: Totals in budgeting, sales, or totals of survey responses. Financial analysts sum expenses or revenue columns.
  Interview Q: “What happens if a cell in SUM’s range is text?” – Model Answer: SUM will ignore text values and only sum numeric cells.
  Common Mistakes: Using + instead of SUM for large ranges; missing the =. Forgetting to extend the range when adding data.
  Tip: Use the AutoSum button (∑) as a shortcut to insert =SUM(...). Always close parentheses if adding more arguments.

AVERAGE

  The AVERAGE function computes the arithmetic mean. Syntax: =AVERAGE(number1, [number2], …).
  For example, =AVERAGE(2,3,5) returns 3. It ignores empty or text cells.
  Example: =AVERAGE(B1:B5) (if B1–B5 have exam scores, returns the average.)

  Use Case: Finding average sales, test scores, or central tendency of data.
  Common Mistakes: Including headers/labels in range can give wrong result. Dividing by wrong count (use AVERAGE instead of manual sum/count to avoid error).
  Tip: Compare AVERAGE with MEDIAN (average is sensitive to outliers; MEDIAN gives the middle value).

COUNT, MAX, MIN, etc.

  Other basic functions like COUNT (counts numbers), MAX, MIN, MEDIAN, MODE are similarly used. Syntax: e.g. =COUNT(A1:A5). They summarize data easily.

Logical Functions: IF, AND, OR

  The IF function returns one value if a condition is true and another if false. Syntax: =IF(logical_test, value_if_true, [value_if_false]).
  For example, =IF(A1>100,"High","Low") checks if A1>100. IF can be nested for multiple conditions.
  Example:=IF(C2="Yes", 1, 0)
             (Returns 1 if cell C2 is Yes, otherwise 0.)

  Use Case: Categorizing data (pass/fail, approval flags). Creating conditional labels (e.g. “Over Budget” vs “OK”).
  Common Mistakes: Forgetting to put text in quotes. Not providing both outcomes (value_if_false is optional but missing it may return 0).
  Tip: For multiple criteria, combine with AND/OR: e.g. =IF(AND(A1>0,B1<100),"Yes","No").

  The AND and OR functions test multiple conditions. 
  AND: returns TRUE only if all conditions are true. 
  OR: returns TRUE if any condition is true. 
  Syntax: =AND(cond1, cond2, …), =OR(cond1, cond2, …).

Lookup Functions: VLOOKUP, INDEX/MATCH

  The VLOOKUP function searches for a value in the leftmost column of a table and returns a value in the same row from a specified column.
  Syntax: =VLOOKUP(lookup_value, table_array, col_index_num, [range_lookup]). 
  For example, =VLOOKUP("ID123", A2:E100, 3, FALSE) finds “ID123” in A2:A100 and returns the corresponding entry from column C (3rd column).

  Use Case: Merging data from different sheets by matching IDs or names. Common in combining lookup tables or pulling reference data.
  Common Mistakes: Data to lookup must be sorted if using TRUE (approximate match). VLOOKUP only searches leftmost column; for more flexibility use INDEX/MATCH.
  Tip: Use FALSE (or 0) for exact match to avoid wrong results. INDEX/MATCH handles lookups in any direction and is more robust.

Data Cleaning Functions: TRIM, CLEAN, etc.

  Functions like TRIM(text) remove extra spaces, and CLEAN(text) removes non-printable characters. 
  Useful for preparing imported data. For example, =TRIM(A1) ensures no leading/trailing spaces.

  Use Case: Cleaning messy CSV or copy-pasted data before analysis.
  Common Mistakes: Forgetting to replace original data; better to output cleaned data to new column to verify.

Charts in Excel

  Excel supports many chart types (bar, line, pie, scatter, etc.).
  For example, a Line Chart shows trends over time – it “shows changes in value across continuous measurements, such as those made over time”. 
  A Scatter Plot displays paired numerical data as points, revealing correlations. Bar charts compare categories by bar height.

  Use Case: Visualizing sales trends, distribution of data, comparisons between categories.
  Tip: Always label axes and provide a title. For dashboards, use combinations (small multiples, or pivot charts).

Pivot Tables

  A PivotTable is a powerful summarization tool: it “calculates, summarizes, and analyzes data” to reveal patterns and trends. 
  It lets you drag fields into rows, columns, and values to group and aggregate data without manual formulas.

  Example Workflow: Insert → PivotTable, select data range, then add fields (e.g. Sales by Region and Quarter).

  Use Case: Quickly summarizing large data sets, e.g. totaling sales by region and year, counting categories, or finding averages.
  Common Mistakes: Not using Excel Tables or named ranges can cause the pivot not to update with new data.
  Tip: Refresh pivot tables after data changes. Use slicers and filters in Excel or Power BI for interactivity.

Conditional Formatting

  Conditional formatting highlights cells based on rules (e.g. color negative numbers red). 
  Use “Highlight Cell Rules” or “Data Bars.” It helps spot outliers or trends.
